    <div class="prose resume-prose">
      <h2>Research Interests</h2>
      <p>
        Understanding how properties of training and evaluation data shape model behaviour, and turning that
        understanding into reliable measurement. My current work isolates how much of a model's apparent
        group-level bias is a real property of the construct being measured versus an artifact of the annotation
        pipeline that produced the data. I want to extend this data-centric, measurement-first approach to LLMs
        &mdash; specifically to factuality and reliable information synthesis, where the same question
        (&ldquo;is this behaviour in the model, or in the data that made it?&rdquo;) is largely unresolved.
      </p>

      <h2>Education</h2>
      <div class="resume-entry">
        <div class="resume-entry-head">
          <h3>Indraprastha Institute of Information Technology, Delhi (IIIT-Delhi)</h3>
          <span class="resume-dates">Dec 2021 &ndash; Jul 2025</span>
        </div>
        <p class="resume-subhead">B.Tech, Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence &middot; CGPA 8.5/10</p>
        <p class="resume-note">
          Full merit scholarship (ICCR, Government of India) &mdash; awarded to international students for
          academic excellence.
        </p>
        <p>
          Relevant coursework: Information Retrieval (A&minus;), Large Language Models (A&minus;), Statistical
          Inference, Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning; Data Structures &amp; Algorithms, Databases,
          Operating Systems, Computer Networks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Research Experience</h2>
      <div class="resume-entry">
        <div class="resume-entry-head">
          <h3>Undergraduate Researcher &mdash; Human-Machine Interaction (HMI) Lab, IIIT-Delhi</h3>
          <span class="resume-dates">2025 &ndash; Present</span>
        </div>
        <p class="resume-note">
          Advisor: Prof. Jainendra Shukla &middot; First-author manuscript in preparation (target: PNAS Nexus)
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Disentangling model bias from measurement bias in automated facial coding.</strong>
          Preregistered study testing whether emotion &rarr; facial Action Unit (AU) mappings are invariant across
          demographic groups, using the RADIATE and NimStim corpora.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Data-centric attribution.</strong> Decomposed apparent demographic bias in automated AU
            detectors and showed it is dominated by an <em>additive per-subject offset</em> rather than by
            group-dependent structure &mdash; i.e. the bias lives in a stable, correctable property of how each
            subject is encoded, not in the emotion&ndash;AU mapping itself. This reframes a &ldquo;model is
            biased&rdquo; claim as a measurable, attributable data artifact.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Multi-detector replication as a reliability gate.</strong> Independently replicated the full
            analysis across three detectors (OpenFace 2.2, Py-Feat, LibreFace 2.0). Found that AU invariance
            across demographic groups holds consistently <em>despite weak raw inter-detector agreement</em>
            &mdash; establishing that the substantive conclusion is a property of the signal, not of any one
            measurement tool.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Statistical pipeline.</strong> Built the end-to-end analysis in Python: generalised linear
            mixed models (statsmodels GLMMs), subject-median centering, subject-stratified cross-validation,
            nonparametric permutation testing, and a preregistered three-criterion decision rule (fold-majority
            outperformance, permutation-null threshold, practical-significance floor).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Confound identification.</strong> Diagnosed and corrected a design flaw in the original
            leave-one-dataset-out protocol in which dataset identity and ethnicity were perfectly collinear,
            making the original effect uninterpretable. Redesigned the validation scheme, which materially
            changed the study's conclusion.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

